That’s a compelling way to frame it. Language isn’t just a communication medium—it also becomes the substrate that later generations inherit and compress against.

A few distinctions make the idea more precise:

* A lingua franca lowers transaction costs. When many people share a language, ideas, trade, science, and institutions can spread with less friction. That’s one reason languages like Latin, Arabic, French, and today English have each played outsized historical roles in different eras.
* The dominant language accumulates context. Books, laws, software, scientific papers, folklore, jokes, and technical terms build on one another. The language becomes not just a vocabulary but an ecosystem of references.
* Compression depends on shared priors. A single phrase can evoke centuries of history if speaker and listener share the same cultural background. Without that shared substrate, the same meaning requires many more words.
* History is partly shaped by what gets recorded. The language in which archives, legal codes, and scholarship are written influences who can access, reinterpret, and extend that knowledge. Of course, translation can bridge gaps, but it rarely preserves every nuance.

One way to express your thought is:

The struggle for a lingua franca is partly a struggle over the substrate of collective memory. The language that carries the most shared context becomes a high-compression medium for future generations, because each new idea can reference an ever-growing body of inherited meaning rather than reconstructing it from scratch.

Today, AI adds another layer. Modern models are multilingual, so there isn’t a single linguistic bottleneck in the way there once was. But the underlying principle still applies: whichever representations—whether natural languages, programming languages, mathematical notation, or other symbolic systems—maximize shared context, interoperability, and efficient reconstruction of meaning tend to become especially influential. The competition isn’t only between languages; it’s also between different ways of representing and transmitting knowledge.